  },
  "locales": {
    "en": {
      "title": "What cases do",
      "summary": "A case shows the job a word does; English keeps cases only in pronouns.",
      "status": "draft",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "What it is",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "A case shows the job that a noun or pronoun does in a sentence: subject, object or owner. English has almost lost its case endings."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "**I** see him. (subject)",
                "He sees **me**. (object)",
                "This is **my** book. (owner)"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "How it works in English",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "Only pronouns keep three cases: subject, object and possessive. Nouns have just one change, the possessive 's. For nouns, word order tells you the subject and the object."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "html": "<table><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Pronoun</th><th>Noun</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>subject</td><td><strong>I, he, they</strong></td><td><strong>the dog</strong></td></tr><tr><td>object</td><td><strong>me, him, them</strong></td><td><strong>the dog</strong></td></tr><tr><td>possessive</td><td><strong>my, his, their</strong></td><td><strong>the dog's</strong></td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
